      <div class="description-content">
        <div id="design-background" class="content-card">
          <h2>Background</h2>

          <div id="design-why-iav">
            <h3>Why detect influenza A virus?</h3>
            <p>Influenza A virus (IAV) poses persistent global public-health threats. According to WHO estimates,
              seasonal influenza causes approximately 1 billion infections, 3-5 million cases of severe illness, and
              290,000-650,000 respiratory deaths worldwide each year, especially among children, the elderly and
              immunocompromised populations. Influenza A virus is highly variable: its surface hemagglutinin (HA)
              protein continuously escapes existing population immunity through antigenic drift and antigenic shift<sup>1</sup>.</p>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_01.png"
                   alt="Figure 1. Transmission routes of influenza A virus and typical high-risk indoor scenarios for airborne IAV exposure">
              <figcaption>Figure 1. Transmission routes of influenza A virus and typical high-risk indoor scenarios for airborne IAV exposure</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>Traditional influenza surveillance mainly relies on &ldquo;patient-centered sampling&rdquo;: swabs from
              symptomatic patients are collected for laboratory testing. This workflow only reflects infections after
              people develop clinical symptoms. Once patients show fever or cough, viruses may already spread widely in
              shared indoor spaces. Early warning before large-scale transmission is hard to achieve by human-centered
              monitoring alone<sup>2</sup>.</p>
            <p>Accumulated evidence confirms that aerosol transmission is a critical route for IAV spread in crowded
              indoor environments, such as schools, hospitals, nursing homes, subway stations and dormitories<sup>3</sup>.
              Virus-carrying fine aerosol particles can stay suspended in air for hours, infecting people via inhalation
              even without close contact with patients<sup>4</sup>.</p>
            <p>Air-borne pathogen monitoring brings unique public-health value:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Pre-symptom early warning:</strong> Detect viral hazards in indoor air before mass symptomatic
                cases emerge, enabling proactive ventilation, disinfection and crowd-flow management, rather than only
                responding after infections happen.</li>
              <li><strong>Identify transmission hot-spots:</strong> Distinguish high-risk locations inside buildings
                (classrooms, wards, waiting halls) and guide targeted prevention measures.</li>
              <li><strong>Complement existing surveillance system:</strong> Current national influenza networks focus on
                clinical patients. Air monitoring adds environmental dimension data to support epidemic trend assessment
                for public-health decision-making.</li>
              <li><strong>Protect high-density congregate settings:</strong> Schools and nursing homes suffer heavy
                influenza-related absences and severe-case burdens. Real-time air risk evaluation helps reduce
                cluster-outbreak risks for vulnerable groups.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div id="design-challenges">
            <h3>Challenges in Airborne Influenza A Virus Monitoring</h3>
            <p>Detecting IAV in air faces intrinsic physical difficulty: viral particles are highly diluted in ambient
              air, with extremely low actual concentration. Today's mainstream paradigm for airborne-virus detection is
              exemplified by bio-aerosol sampling combined with quantitative reverse-transcription PCR (qRT-PCR), the
              gold-standard laboratory method. This workflow is divided into two discrete steps: bio-aerosol samples are
              first collected on-site and then transported back to a central laboratory for qRT-PCR analysis. No mature,
              low-cost on-site POCT solution exists for air-borne IAV surveillance<sup>5</sup>.</p>
          </div>

