        <div id="design-solution" class="content-card">
          <h2>Solution</h2>

          <div id="design-overall">
            <h3>Overall Design</h3>
            <p>To address this Challenge, our team designed an engineered yeast whole-cell biosensor that aims to achieve
              low-cost, high-sensitivity, field-deployable detection of airborne influenza A virus. Our system adopts the
              PAGER (Protein-fragment Assisted GPCR Expression Reporter) programmable antigen-gated receptor strategy,
              adapted from a published synthetic GPCR framework<sup>1</sup>. In the presence of the hemagglutinin (HA)
              antigen from influenza A virus, the receptor is de-repressed, thereby triggering the downstream yeast MAPK
              mating pathway and activating reporter gene expression to produce a fluorescent readout.</p>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_02.png"
                   alt="Figure 2. Modular Design of the Engineered Yeast Biosensor">
              <figcaption>Figure 2. Modular Design of the Engineered Yeast Biosensor. The chassis is rewired for GPCR-MAPK coupling; the PAGER receptor enables HA-gated de-repression; the FUS1-driven cassette provides reporter output.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>Our project follows a modular design strategy:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Chassis Cell Engineering.</strong> FAR1, and SST2 are knocked out to remove endogenous
                interference, prevent cell cycle arrest, and enhance signal sensitivity<sup>2,3</sup>.</li>
              <li><strong>Humanized Gpa1-G&alpha;q chimeric G protein.</strong> The yeast Gpa1 C-terminus is replaced
                with the human G&alpha;q C-terminus to couple hM1Dq to the yeast MAPK pathway<sup>4</sup>.</li>
              <li><strong>PAGER antigen-gated fusion receptor protein design for HA recognition.</strong> hM1Dq is a
                human M1 DREADD receptor that can be activated by DCZ<sup>1</sup>. Anti-IAV HA nanobody (VHH)
                specifically binds H1N1 or H3N2 HA<sup>5</sup>. MT1 muscarinic toxin acts as an auto-inhibitory peptide
                that occupies the orthosteric site of the hM1Dq receptor. Antigen binding relieves this inhibition via
                steric hindrance<sup>1</sup>.</li>
              <li><strong>FUS1-driven reporter output module.</strong> The yeast FUS1 promoter drives yEGFP or MEL1
                expression, producing green fluorescence or blue color as the output signal<sup>6</sup>.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Once these four modules are assembled and validated, the sensor will be further interfaced with an air
              sampler (e.g., a cyclone sampler or impactor) to collect airborne particles into a liquid medium. The
              collected liquid is then applied to the yeast sensor, enabling airborne influenza A virus detection.</p>
          </div>

          <div id="design-module1">
            <h3>Module 1: Chassis Cell Engineering — Knockout of FAR1 and SST2</h3>
            <h4>Design Purpose</h4>
            <p>Wild-type <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> BY4741 possesses a native mating pheromone response MAPK
              pathway. Two endogenous factors interfere with our synthetic human-origin GPCR (hM1Dq) signal transmission:</p>
            <p><strong>Cell-cycle arrest:</strong> pheromone stimulation triggers Far1-mediated G1 arrest, preventing
              continuous proliferation and long-term real-time monitoring<sup>2</sup>.</p>
            <p><strong>Negative regulation:</strong> Sst2 acts as a negative regulator of G-protein signaling, dampening
              the signal amplitude of the GPCR&ndash;MAPK cascade<sup>3</sup>.</p>
            <p>To re-wire the native pathway for our synthetic sensor, we perform targeted gene editing to remove growth
              arrest and enhance signal sensitivity, creating a chassis better suited for downstream human GPCR coupling.</p>
            <h4>Gene Knockout Design</h4>
            <p>We use the CRISPR-Cas9 system to introduce frameshift mutations in two target genes, FAR1 and SST2, in
              BY4741. sgRNAs targeting the coding sequences of these genes are cloned into the pML104 vector, which
              carries both the Cas9 expression cassette and the sgRNA expression cassette. After transformation, Cas9 is
              guided by the sgRNAs to cleave the target loci, and the resulting double-strand breaks are repaired by
              non-homologous end joining (NHEJ), frequently generating frameshift mutations that disrupt gene function.
              Correctly edited clones will be verified by colony PCR and Sanger sequencing.</p>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_03.jpeg"
                   alt="Figure 3. Signal Transduction Mechanism and Chassis Optimization of the Engineered Yeast Biosensor">
              <figcaption>Figure 3. Signal Transduction Mechanism and Chassis Optimization of the Engineered Yeast Biosensor</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>

          <div id="design-module2">
            <h3>Module 2: Humanized G-Protein Engineering — Gpa1-G&alpha;q Chimera</h3>
            <h4>Design Purpose</h4>
            <p>Native yeast Gpa1 (the G&alpha; subunit) cannot recognize the human-derived hM1Dq GPCR. To couple hM1Dq to
              the yeast intracellular MAPK pathway, we need to engineer the G&alpha; subunit so that it can be recognized
              and activated by hM1Dq. Literature indicates that the C-terminal 5 amino acids of the G&alpha; protein
              determine GPCR&ndash;G&alpha; recognition specificity. Replacing the C-terminus of yeast Gpa1 with the
              corresponding human G&alpha;q sequence is sufficient to change its receptor coupling specificity while
              preserving its ability to activate the yeast downstream pathway<sup>4</sup>.</p>
            <h4>Gpa1&ndash;G&alpha;q chimeric protein design</h4>
            <p>Native yeast Gpa1 (G&alpha; subunit) cannot recognize human-derived hM1Dq GPCR. Literature indicates that
              C-terminal 5 amino acids of G&alpha; protein determine GPCR&ndash;G&alpha; recognition specificity<sup>4</sup>.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Wild-type yeast Gpa1 C-terminal 5 aa: &ldquo;KIGII&rdquo;</li>
              <li>Replace with human G&alpha;q C-terminal 5 aa: &ldquo;EYNLV&rdquo;</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The chimeric Gpa1-G&alpha;q retains the yeast Gpa1 backbone, which is capable of activating the yeast
              G&beta;&gamma; (Ste4/Ste18) and downstream Ste5/Ste7/Fus3 MAPK cascade, but its C-terminus is now
              compatible with human hM1Dq.</p>
            <p>To humanize the C-terminus of yeast Gpa1, a donor cassette containing the upstream homology arm
              (Homology-U), the URA3 selectable marker, and the downstream homology arm (Homology-D) was designed, with
              the humanized 5-amino-acid sequence EYNLV followed by a stop codon placed between the two homology arms.
              This cassette was introduced into <i>S. cerevisiae</i> by transformation, where homologous recombination
              directed its integration into the native GPA1 locus on Chromosome VIII (region 116,000&ndash;111,500 bp;
              NCBI RefSeq: GCF_000146045.2), replacing the original C-terminal motif KIGII. Correctly edited clones were
              selected on SD-Ura medium and verified by colony PCR and Sanger sequencing, yielding the Gpa1-G&alpha;q
              chimera for functional coupling with hM1Dq.</p>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_04.png"
                   alt="Figure 4. Schematic of GPA1 Locus Modification and Humanized Sequence Integration via Homologous Recombination">
              <figcaption>Figure 4. Schematic of GPA1 Locus Modification and Humanized Sequence Integration via Homologous Recombination</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>

          <div id="design-module3">
            <h3>Module 3: PAGER Antigen&#8209;Gated Fusion Receptor Protein Design</h3>
            <h4>Design Purpose</h4>
            <p>We adopt PAGER synthetic GPCR &ldquo;antigen-gated de-inhibition&rdquo; logic. In absence of target HA
              antigen, receptor stays self-inhibited with very low background signal. When influenza A HA antigen appears,
              antigen-nanobody binding relieves self-inhibition, enabling downstream signal activation upon DCZ
              small-molecule agonist stimulation<sup>1</sup>.</p>
            <p>Our full-length fusion protein domain arrangement (N-terminal to C-terminal): MT1 auto-inhibitory
              peptide &ndash; anti-HA nanobody &ndash; TEV protease cleavage site &ndash; ALFA tag &ndash; hM1Dq receptor.</p>
            <h4>Each domain function:</h4>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>MT1 auto-inhibitory toxin peptide:</strong> Constitutive suppressor domain for hM1Dq. Without
                HA antigen, MT1 binds hM1Dq and locks receptor in inactive state to minimize background leakage
                signal<sup>1</sup>.</li>
              <li><strong>anti-HA nanobody:</strong> Recognition element, high-specific affinity binding to hemagglutinin
                HA of influenza A virus. When HA binds nanobody, steric hindrance displaces MT1 peptide away from hM1Dq,
                releasing receptor inhibition<sup>5</sup>.</li>
              <li><strong>TEV protease cleavage site:</strong> Built-in positive-control switch. TEV protease cleavage
                can artificially remove MT1 inhibition, to validate whether downstream signaling cascade works normally.</li>
              <li><strong>hM1Dq human-derived GPCR:</strong> Core receptor. After de-repression, small-molecule agonist
                DCZ can activate hM1Dq, which transduces intracellular signals through our Gpa1-G&alpha;q chimera to
                trigger the yeast MAPK pathway<sup>1</sup>.</li>
            </ol>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_05.png"
                   alt="Figure 5. Schematic Representation of the Structural Composition and Signal Transduction of the Engineered PAGER Receptor">
              <figcaption>Figure 5. Schematic Representation of the Structural Composition and Signal Transduction of the Engineered PAGER Receptor</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>

          <div id="design-module4">
            <h3>Module 4: Reporter Output Module: FUS1 Promoter Reporter System</h3>
            <p>After the MAPK signaling cascade is activated, a readable output signal is required for biosensor readout.
              We selected the native yeast FUS1 promoter, which is natively triggered by the yeast mating-pheromone MAPK
              cascade. Once the upstream receptor is activated, FUS1 initiates transcription of the downstream reporter
              gene<sup>6</sup>.</p>
            <p>We built two independent single-reporter plasmids for parallel testing. One produces fluorescent output
              (yEGFP), the other produces colorimetric output (MEL1 /&alpha;-galactosidase with X-&alpha;-Gal
              substrate). By comparing signal intensity, background noise, dynamic range and experimental operability
              between the two systems, we aim to identify the more suitable readout strategy for our yeast biosensor<sup>7</sup>.</p>
            <p><strong>Plasmid backbone:</strong> pESC-HIS. Two constructed reporter plasmids:</p>
            <p>pESC-HIS-FUS1-yEGFP: Fluorescent output, quantitative measurement via fluorescence detector.</p>
            <p>pESC-HIS-FUS1-MEL1: Colorimetric output, chromogenic reaction with X-&alpha;-Gal substrate.</p>
            <p><strong>Experimental goal:</strong> Evaluate and select the optimal read-out modality for our biosensor
              from fluorescence or colorimetric output.</p>

            <figure class="design-figure">
              <img loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   src="../static/image/wait/design_06.png"
                   alt="Figure 6. Two readout reporter circuits driven by FUS1 promoter in yeast">
              <figcaption>Figure 6. Two readout reporter circuits driven by FUS1 promoter in yeast</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>

          <div id="design-safety">
            <h3>Safety &amp; Design Considerations</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Chassis biosafety:</strong> In this study, the non-pathogenic laboratory standard strain
                <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> BY4741 was selected as the chassis cell. This strain belongs to
                Biosafety Level 1 (BSL-1) microorganisms and poses no pathogenic risk. In terms of biosafety design, we
                exploit its auxotrophic properties to construct a biological containment barrier, thereby reducing the
                risk of uncontrolled dissemination of the engineered strain in natural environments. BY4741 is naturally
                auxotrophic for histidine (His), leucine (Leu), methionine (Met), and uracil (Ura). The PAGER system, the
                reporter system, and the plasmids for Gpa1 humanization and gene knockout constructed in this study carry
                LEU, HIS, and URA selection markers, respectively, which complement the corresponding auxotrophies. To
                ensure biosafety, we further designed a marker-removal strategy: the URA marker will be removed by
                5-fluoroorotic acid (5-FOA) counter-selection, so that the engineered strain will ultimately maintain a
                double auxotrophy for uracil and methionine. This double-auxotrophy mechanism is designed to render the
                strain unable to proliferate effectively outside the specific supplemented medium, thereby effectively
                reducing the risk of environmental release.</li>
              <li><strong>Recognition element:</strong> anti-HA nanobody and hM1Dq receptor are heterologous protein
                expression; no virulence-related genes are introduced. No replication-competent viral sequences are used
                in our constructs.</li>
              <li><strong>Control logic:</strong> Self-inhibition design greatly reduces constitutive leakage activation,
                lowering false-positive risk of sensor and improving the signal-to-noise ratio of our biosensor.</li>
              <li><strong>Modularity design:</strong> Our whole system is modularized. Only replacing the nanobody domain
                can change detection target, supporting future adaptation to other respiratory pathogens such as RSV or
                coronavirus.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

